+++
title: Pasargadae
description: Archaeological site in Fars province, Iran
hook: There is no firm evidence that the tomb at the heart of Pasargadae holds
  Cyrus the Great. Greek historians say Alexander believed it did, and that he
  came to see it after he had looted and destroyed Persepolis.
standfirst: Cyrus the Great founded Pasargadae in the 6th century BCE as the
  first capital of the Achaemenid Empire, near the site of his victory over
  the Median king Astyages in 550 BCE. It lies in ruins in Fars province,
  Iran, 40 kilometers from Persepolis.
key-facts:
  - name: Built
    value: 6th century BCE, by Cyrus the Great
    from: "history: built: 6th century BCE built-by: Cyrus the Great"
  - name: Where
    value: Fars province, Iran — about 90 kilometres (56 mi) northeast of Shiraz
    from: located just north of the town of [[ Madar-e-Soleyman ]] and about 90
      kilometres (56 mi) to the northeast of the city of [[ Shiraz ]]
  - name: World Heritage listing
    value: Inscribed 2004 (28th Session), reference 1106, on cultural criteria
      (i), (ii), (iii), (iv)
    from: 'unesco-world-heritage-site: criteria: "Cultural: (i), (ii), (iii),
      (iv)" reference: "1106" inscription: 2004 (28th Session)'
  - name: How big
    value: 160 ha (0.62 sq mi), inside a buffer zone of 7,127 ha (27.52 sq mi)
    from: "area: 160 ha (0.62 sq mi) buffer-zone: 7,127 ha (27.52 sq mi)"
  - name: What is left
    value: Stone and clay, in ruins
    from: "site-notes: material: Stone, clay archaeologists: Ali Sami, David
      Stronach, Ernst Herzfeld condition: In ruins"
  - name: Dug by
    value: Ali Sami, David Stronach, Ernst Herzfeld
    from: "archaeologists: Ali Sami, David Stronach, Ernst Herzfeld"
pull-quotes:
  - text: Passer-by, I am Cyrus, who gave the Persians an empire, and was king of
      Asia. Grudge me not therefore this monument.
    attribution: The inscription on the tomb, as Strabo and Arrian report it; no
      trace of any such inscription survives
    section: tomb-of-cyrus-the-great
  - text: I am Cyrus the king, an Achaemenid.
    attribution: Carved in a column in Pasargadae, in Old Persian, Elamite and
      Akkadian languages
    section: tomb-of-cyrus-the-great
timeline:
  - when: 559–530 BC
    what: Pasargadae is the capital of the Achaemenid Empire under Cyrus the Great.
    from: was the capital of the [[ Achaemenid Empire ]] under [[ Cyrus the Great
      ]] (559–530 BC)
  - when: 550 BCE
    what: Cyrus defeats the Median king Astyages; Pasargadae is founded near the
      site of the victory, in the 6th century BCE, as the empire's first
      capital, and named after his royal clan.
    from: Pasargadae was founded in the 6th century BCE as the first capital of
      the [[ Achaemenid Empire ]] by [[ Cyrus the Great ]], near the site of
      his victory over the [[ Median | medes ]] king [[ Astyages ]] in 550
      BCE. The city is named after his own royal clan.[[ ^3 ]]
  - when: 5th-4th centuries BC
    what: Date of the gold and gem jewellery later found as the Pasargadae
      Treasure.
    from: Dating to the 5th-4th centuries BC, the treasure consists of ornate [[
      Achaemenid | achaemenid-art ]] jewellery made from gold and precious
      gems
  - when: second century CE
    what: Arrian records that Alexander sent Aristobulus into the tomb.
    from: "[[ Arrian ]] recorded in the second century CE that Alexander commanded
      Aristobulus, one of his warriors, to enter the monument."
  - when: "1840"
    what: Eugène Flandin draws the tomb of Cyrus the Great.
    from: The tomb of Cyrus the Great by [[ Eugène Flandin ]], 1840
  - when: "1905"
    what: Ernst Herzfeld makes the first archaeological exploration of the site.
    from: Pasargadae was first archaeologically explored by the German
      archaeologist [[ Ernst Herzfeld ]] in 1905
  - when: "1928"
    what: Herzfeld digs a single excavation season with his assistant Friedrich
      Krefter.
    from: and in one excavation season in 1928, together with his assistant
      Friedrich Krefter.[[ ^12 ]]
  - when: "1930"
    what: Manuel Bandeira publishes "Vou-me embora pra Pasárgada" in
      //Libertinagem//.
    from: In 1930, the [[ Brazilian | brazil ]] poet [[ Manuel Bandeira ]]
      published a poem called "Vou-me embora pra Pasárgada" ("I'm off to
      Pasargadae" in [[ Portuguese | portuguese-language ]]), in a book
      entitled //Libertinagem//.[[ ^28 ]]
  - when: 1931-09-16
    what: Pasargadae is designated Iranian National Heritage.
    from: 'heritage-designation: - value: World Heritage Site from: "2004" -
      value: Iranian National Heritage from: 1931-09-16'
  - when: "1934"
    what: Sir Aurel Stein completes a site plan.
    from: After Herzfeld, [[ Sir Aurel Stein ]] completed a site plan for
      Pasargadae in 1934.[[ ^13 ]]
  - when: "1935"
    what: Erich F. Schmidt photographs the entire complex from the air.
    from: In 1935, Erich F. Schmidt produced a series of aerial photographs of the
      entire complex.[[ ^14 ]]
  - when: "1946"
    what: The papers, photographs and finds of the early excavations go to the
      Freer Gallery of Art in Washington, DC.
    from: Since 1946, the original documents, notebooks, photographs, fragments of
      wall paintings and pottery from the early excavations are preserved in
      the [[ Freer Gallery of Art ]], Smithsonian Institution, in Washington,
      DC.
  - when: 1949 to 1955
    what: An Iranian team led by Ali Sami works at the site.
    from: From 1949 to 1955, an Iranian team led by Ali Sami worked there.[[ ^15 ]]
  - when: 1961 to 1963
    what: A British Institute of Persian Studies team led by David Stronach
      resumes excavation.
    from: A British Institute of Persian Studies team led by [[ David Stronach ]]
      resumed excavation from 1961 to 1963.[[ ^16 ]][[ ^17 ]][[ ^18 ]]
  - when: the 1960s
    what: The pot-hoard known as the Pasargadae Treasure is excavated near the
      foundations of 'Pavilion B'.
    from: It was during the 1960s that a pot-hoard known as the Pasargadae
      Treasure was excavated near the foundations of 'Pavilion B' at the site.
  - when: "2000"
    what: Work resumes under the Iranian Cultural Heritage Organization and the
      Maison de l'Orient et de la Méditerranée of the University of Lyon.
    from: After a gap, work was resumed by the Iranian Cultural Heritage
      Organization and the [[ Maison de l'Orient et de la Méditerranée ]] of
      the [[ University of Lyon ]] in 2000.[[ ^21 ]][[ ^22 ]]
  - when: 2004 (28th Session)
    what: Pasargadae is inscribed as a World Heritage Site on cultural criteria
      (i), (ii), (iii), (iv).
    from: 'unesco-world-heritage-site: criteria: "Cultural: (i), (ii), (iii),
      (iv)" reference: "1106" inscription: 2004 (28th Session)'
  - when: "2006"
    what: The remains found near Toll-e Takht are identified as the tomb of
      Cambyses II.
    from: The remains of the [[ tomb of Cyrus ]]' son and successor [[ Cambyses II
      ]] have been found in Pasargadae, near the fortress of Toll-e Takht, and
      identified in 2006.[[ ^5 ]]
  - when: 19 April 2007
    what: Construction of the Sivand Dam begins, with the waterline height limited
      to mitigate damage to the ruins.
    from: Construction of the dam began on 19 April 2007, with the height of the
      waterline limited so as to mitigate damage to the ruins.[[ ^27 ]]
