    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: "The [[ Tomb of Cyrus ]] the Great: six broad steps up to a chamber
      3.17 metres (10.4 ft) long, entered by a low and narrow door."
  - file: Pasargadae_CyrustheGreatTomb_22061.jpg
    role: full
    section: history
    caption: "[[ Pasargad ]] seen from Toll-e Takht hill. The nearest building is
      the tomb attributed to [[ Cambyses II ]]; the farthest, in the centre of
      the upper part of the image, is the [[ Tomb of Cyrus ]] the Great.
      Between the two lie the ruins of Achaemenid palaces."
  - file: I_am_Cyrus,_Achaemenid_King_-_Pasargadae.JPG
    role: inline
    section: tomb-of-cyrus-the-great
    caption: '"I am [[ Cyrus | cyrus-the-great ]] the king, an [[ Achaemenid ]]."
      — carved in a column in Pasargadae in [[ Old Persian ]], [[ Elamite |
      elamite-language ]] and [[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]] languages.'
  - file: Pasargadae_Swallow-Tail_Staples.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: "[[ Dovetail ]] [[ staples | staple-fastener ]] from Pasargadae,
      where the architecture showed elements of Elamite and Egyptian or
      Phoenician decoration as well as [[ Ionian | ionia ]] Greek building
      techniques.[[ ^11 ]]"
  - file: Passargade_by_Eugène_Flandin.jpg
    role: inline
    section: in-popular-culture
    caption: The tomb of Cyrus the Great as drawn by [[ Eugène Flandin ]] in 1840,
      ninety years before Manuel Bandeira put the name into a poem.[[ ^28 ]]
  - file: Flag_of_Iran.svg
    role: drop
    section: lead
    caption: Flag of Iran — a national flag that shows nothing of the site.
glossary:
  - term: propylaeum
    gloss: A monumental gateway; Gate R at Pasargadae is the oldest known
      freestanding one, and may have been the architectural predecessor of the
      Gate of All Nations at Persepolis.
    from: The Gate R, located at the eastern edge of the palace area, is the
      oldest known freestanding [[ propylaeum ]]. It may have been the
      architectural predecessor of the [[ Gate of All Nations ]] at [[
      Persepolis ]].[[ ^6 ]]
  - term: chahar bagh
    gloss: The Persian fourfold garden design; the Pasargadae Persian Gardens
      provide its earliest known example.
    from: Pasargadae Persian Gardens provide the earliest known example of the
      Persian //[[ chahar bagh | charbagh ]]//, or fourfold garden design (see
      [[ Persian Gardens ]]).
  - term: cella
    gloss: The element of Cyrus' tomb usually attributed to Urartu tombs of an
      earlier period, unlike the design as a whole, which is credited to
      Mesopotamian or Elamite ziggurats.
    from: The design of Cyrus' tomb is credited to Mesopotamian or Elamite [[
      ziggurats | ziggurat ]], but the [[ cella ]] is usually attributed to [[
      Urartu ]] tombs of an earlier period.[[ ^8 ]]
  - term: sepulchre
    gloss: The burial chamber of the tomb, reached by six broad steps and
      measuring 3.17 metres (10.4 ft) long by 2.11 metres (6 ft 11 in) wide by
      2.11 metres (6 ft 11 in) high.
    from: It has six broad steps leading to the sepulchre, the chamber of which
      measures 3.17 metres (10.4 ft) long by 2.11 metres (6 ft 11 in) wide by
      2.11 metres (6 ft 11 in) high and has a low and narrow entrance.
  - term: Toll-e Takht
    gloss: The fortress on a hill near the tomb, about three kilometres from it,
      at the far end of the site's pattern of gardens; also spelled Tall-i
      Takht.
    from: the palace complex was constituted by an extensive pattern of gardens
      stretching from the Tomb of Cyrus to a fortress known as the Tall-i
      Takht, about three kilometres away, and containing various palaces and
      other buildings
  - term: Pasargadae Treasure
    gloss: A pot-hoard of ornate Achaemenid jewellery in gold and precious gems,
      dating to the 5th-4th centuries BC, now in the National Museum of Iran
      and the British Museum.
    from: a pot-hoard known as the Pasargadae Treasure was excavated near the
      foundations of 'Pavilion B' at the site. Dating to the 5th-4th centuries
      BC, the treasure consists of ornate [[ Achaemenid | achaemenid-art ]]
      jewellery made from gold and precious gems and is now housed in the [[
      National Museum of Iran ]] and the [[ British Museum ]].[[ ^19 ]]
  - term: Madar-e-Soleyman
    gloss: '"Mother of Solomon" — the figure the tomb was previously attributed
      to, and the name of the town just south of the site.'
    from: It is considered to be the location of the [[ Tomb of Cyrus ]], a tomb
      previously attributed to //Madar-e-Soleyman//, the "Mother of Solomon".
  - term: Achaemenid Empire
    gloss: The empire ruled by Cyrus the Great (559–530 BC), whose first capital
      was Pasargadae until Darius moved it to Persepolis.
    from: It remained the [[ Achaemenid ]] capital until [[ Darius |
      darius-the-great ]] moved it to [[ Persepolis ]].[[ ^4 ]]
further:
  - slug: persepolis
    why: The capital Darius moved to from Pasargadae, 40 kilometers away, and the
      city Alexander looted and destroyed before visiting Cyrus' tomb.
  - slug: alexander-the-great
    why: Greek historians say he believed the tomb was Cyrus', and sent
      Aristobulus inside to see what was there.
  - slug: babylon
    why: One of the traditions Pasargadae's art and architecture drew on,
      alongside Elam, Assyria and ancient Egypt.
anchors:
  hook: Though there is no firm evidence identifying the tomb as that of Cyrus,
    Greek historians say that [[ Alexander | alexander-the-great ]] believed
    it was. When Alexander looted and destroyed Persepolis, he paid a visit to
    the tomb of Cyrus.
  standfirst: >-
    Pasargadae was founded in the 6th century BCE as the first capital of the
    [[ Achaemenid Empire ]] by [[ Cyrus the Great ]], near the site of his
    victory over the [[ Median | medes ]] king [[ Astyages ]] in 550 BCE.


    The first capital of the [[ Achaemenid Empire ]], Pasargadae lies in ruins
    40 kilometers from [[ Persepolis ]], in present-day [[ Fars province ]] of
    [[ Iran ]].[[ ^10 ]]
